          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described about salt and fluid for someone living with heart failure?",
          "answer": "**What the sources say**\n\n- **Why salt matters in heart failure:** The \"Diet and fluid in heart failure\" note (lifestyle-heart-failure-diet) explains that salt holds water in the body. A heart that cannot pump that extra volume can back fluid up into the lungs and legs. That is why a single high-salt meal can bring on breathlessness within a day or two. The main concern is fluid build-up, not blood pressure.\n- **Where the salt comes from:** The same note says most dietary salt is already in packaged and restaurant food, not added at the table. It refers to the blood-pressure diet note (lifestyle-hypertension-diet) for the details.\n- **Targets:** The note says the commonly stated salt targets are \"in a similar range\" to those for blood pressure. It does not give the numbers.\n\n**What the sources do not cover**\n\n- They do not say how much fluid a person should drink, or give a daily sodium figure.\n- They do not describe a daily weight check, though the note's title mentions \"the daily weight.\"\n- The guideline results I found were about heart failure medicines, not salt or fluid limits. The heart failure guideline I found (guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy) covers drug classes and follow-up after a hospital stay, not diet.\n\n**What to discuss with a clinician**\n\nAsk what salt and fluid limits suit you, and how often to weigh yourself and what weight change should prompt a call. Also ask what symptoms should lead you to contact the team.\n\nIf you have sudden or severe breathlessness, chest pain, fainting, or breathlessness at rest, seek emergency care now. Do not wait for an appointment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
